 i i would recommend many of  the statements that briefs that were  entered in the murray case i think are  the best representation of atheism in  america that you're going to get they're  really very good  so  before i came tonight i was talking with  somebody about your book and they said  madeline murray o'hare gave atheists a  bad name  did she catch much heat from within the  movement  to tone it down and try to become more  you know  mom-and-pop atheism right that's a good  question and it kind of relates back to  maura's question earlier  when she talked about her being as one  of many liberals of the time  her behavior was such that  other liberals  if we can call her a liberal and almost  you know so many people in her movement  rejected her because she was just so  studiedly crude  and vulgar  that people just wouldn't have anything  to do with her at first they were okay  through much of the 60s because that was  kind of a crazy time anyway and  everyone was acting bad uh you know it  was easy in a sense for madeleine she  had lots of cover for her behavior  as you go into the late 70s and in the  80s  in a more  civil time if you want you could take  that as a positive or negative view all  the time um  people really began to just reject her  and whenever she was rejected she tended  to be even more outrageous in her  behavior  one typical scene and it comes you know  she had a great way of mimicking so  people wouldn't miss the point she went  into a bingo  game in a church and did the christ  thing from the new testament went in and  tipped over the tables and started  screaming at the top of her lungs in  order to make the point this was sort of  typical and they did distant that many  people did distance herself from  themselves from her  many times the americans who are  perceived in their own country are  admired and revered abroad for example  woody allen in france marilyn monroe  jerry lewis  roman polanski well he was a european  though how do y